    id: 15,
    title: "Does one sheep suffice for an entire household?",
    question: "Does a sacrificial animal (udhiyah) from sheep suffice for all the members of a household, or only for one person?",
    ruling: `The sacrificial animal is sufficient for the entire household, even if they were one hundred, if it is slaughtered as a sacrifice on behalf of him, his wife, his children, and all his household. It is acceptable even if it is just one sheep, or one cow, or one camel, because the Messenger ﷺ used to offer one sheep as a sacrifice on behalf of himself and the members of his household.

[Fatāwā Nūr ʿalāʾl-darb]

The majority of scholars said that one sheep is sufficient on behalf of the members of a single household. If one member of the household offers it, the ritual and Sunnah are fulfilled for all of them. This is the view of Malik, Al-Shafi'i, Ahmad, Ishaq, and Al-Awza'i. (Al-Mughni 9/438; Al-Majmu' 8/384).

Al-Tirmidhi narrated with his chain from Abu Ayyub (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: "A man would offer a sheep on behalf of himself and the members of his household, and they would eat from it and give some to others, until people later began competing, and the practice became what you see today." Al-Tirmidhi said: This is a sound and authentic hadith.

Regarding intention: it is permissible to make the intention before the time of slaughter — when purchasing the animal is sufficient.

Regarding sacrificing on behalf of others: If someone offers a sacrifice on behalf of another person without that person's permission, the sacrifice does not count for them.

[Fatāwā yasʾalūnaka]`,
